## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design with an extra edit dimension on a classic string problem; requires careful transitions for matches, edits, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings (i, j)
- Extra dimension for edits (t)
- Transitions for match/mismatch with edits
- Path reconstruction and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxLPSWithEdits(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) dp[i][i][t] = 1;
  for (let len=2; len<=n; len++) {
    for (let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++) {
      const j = i+len-1;
      for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
        let best = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t]);
        if (s[i] === s[j]) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t] + 2);
        if (t >= 1) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-1] + 2);
        if (t >= 2) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-2] + 2);
        dp[i][j][t] = best;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to require exactly k edits instead of at most?
- How to reduce space to O(n*k)?
- How to reconstruct the edited string and the subsequence?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates the ability to design a dynamic programming solution for selecting exactly k vertex-disjoint directed paths in a DAG while maximizing total node weights. It is a common pattern in interviews at Netflix, Uber, and Meta.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological ordering** ensures DP processes vertices in valid dependency order
- **DP over path count dimension** (t from 1 to k) tracks the maximum weight for t paths ending at each vertex
- **Vertex-disjoint constraint** is naturally enforced through the state definition, since each path is built incrementally
- **Parent pointer array** enables reconstruction of the selected paths after DP computation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for k vertex-disjoint path selection

function selectKPaths(N, M, edges, weights, k) {
  const dp = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  // Get topological order
  const topo = topologicalSort(N, edges);

  // Initialize sources
  for (const s of sources) {
    dp[s][1] = weights[s];
  }

  // Process in topological order
  for (const u of topo) {
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      for (let t = 1; t < k; t++) {
        if (dp[u][t] > -Infinity) {
          const newWeight = dp[u][t] + weights[v];
          if (newWeight > dp[v][t + 1]) {
            dp[v][t + 1] = newWeight;
            parent[v][t + 1] = u;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct paths using parent pointers
  return reconstructPaths(dp, parent, k);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend DP with a cooldown constraint and to backtrack trades.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-state DP with cooldown
- 1-based transaction counting, backtracking
- Space optimization via rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitWithCooldown(prices, k) {
  const n = prices.length; if (!n || k===0) return 0;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  let no = new Array(k+1).fill(0);
  let hold = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  let cool = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  hold[0] = -prices[0];
  for (let i=1;i<n;i++){
    let nno=no.slice(), nhold=hold.slice(), ncool=cool.slice();
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      nno[t] = Math.max(nno[t], cool[t]);
      nhold[t] = Math.max(nhold[t], no[t] - prices[i]);
      if (t>0) ncool[t] = Math.max(ncool[t], hold[t-1] + prices[i]);
    }
    no=nno; hold=nhold; cool=ncool;
  }
  let best=0; for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) best=Math.max(best,no[t],cool[t]);
  return best;
}
```

