- How would you reconstruct the exact trade days?
- How does the solution change with unlimited cooldown or different cooldown durations?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests a concrete DP pattern for selecting exactly k non-overlapping subarrays with a fixed per-subarray cost. It requires careful state definitions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling of negative sums.

## Key Concepts

- DP for k disjoint subarrays with end-state tracking
- Prefix sums or running sums to compute subarray value fast
- Reconstruction pointers to recover endpoints
- Time: O(nk); Space: O(nk) (with optimizations)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length, NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = NEG;
      if (t > 0) end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], end[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How handle cases where all values are negative or c is large?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP formulation for selecting multiple non-overlapping subarrays with reconstruction—a common pattern in budget allocation and resource optimization scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- **DP over prefixes and block counts**: State `dp[i][t]` tracks the optimal solution using only the first i elements for exactly t blocks.
- **Prefix sums**: Enable O(1) computation of any subarray sum as `pref[r] - pref[l-1]`.
- **Backpointers**: Store decision history to reconstruct the actual subarray boundaries after computing the DP table.
- **Time/space complexity**: Baseline O(n²k) time and O(nk) space; can be optimized with divide-and-conquer or convex hull tricks for larger inputs.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      choice[i][t] = { type: 'skip', i: i - 1 };

      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const val = dp[s - 1][t - 1] + pref[i] - pref[s - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          choice[i][t] = { type: 'start', s, i };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct boundaries
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    const c = choice[i][t];
    if (c.type === 'start') {
      blocks.unshift([c.s, c.i]);
      i = c.s - 1;
    } else {
      i = c.i;
    }
    t--;
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], boundaries: blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a spacing constraint and path reconstruction, a common interview variation.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix with constrained subsequences
- 2D state and reconstruction pointers
- Handling infeasible states and space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// state definitions: dp[i][t], par[i][t]
function solve(A, n, k, d) {
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) dp[i][1] = A[i-1];
  for (let t=2; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
      // skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; par[i][t] = -1; }
      // take i
      if (i - d >= 1 && dp[i-d][t-1] !== NEG) {
        const val = dp[i-d][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = i-d; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the indices from par to return the actual solution?
- How can you optimize space without changing asymptotic time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a range-based segment cost and path reconstruction, a common advanced pattern not covered yet.

## Key Concepts
- Contiguous partitioning
- Range query (max-min)
- DP with backtracking
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  // base
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    let mx = -Infinity, mn = Infinity;
    for (let p = i - 1; p >= 0; p--) {
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[p]);
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[p]);
      dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i)) - Math.min(...A.slice(0, i));
    }
  }
  // transitions would fill dp[i][t] and cut[i][t]
  return { cost: dp[n][k], partition: cut };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize range(p, i-1) queries to O(1) per transition?
- How would you adapt for 1-indexed arrays or allow at most k segments?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to design a dynamic programming solution for fixed-partition subproblems. It requires careful state definition, transition logic, and the ability to handle substring-to-integer conversion efficiently. Additionally, storing cut pointers enables reconstruction of the optimal partition.

## Key Concepts

- **Partition DP**: Divide a linear sequence into exactly k segments
- **On-the-fly value computation**: Accumulate digit values incrementally without re-parsing substrings
- **Reconstruction**: Maintain a parent pointer array to recover the cut positions
- **Time/Space tradeoffs**: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space; can optimize to O(nk) space using rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const digits = s.split('').map(Number);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      let val = 0;
      for (let j = i; j >= t; j--) {
        val = digits[j - 1] * Math.pow(10, i - j) + val;
        if (dp[j - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const sum = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + val;
          if (sum > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = sum;
            cut[i][t] = j - 1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const cuts = [];
  let pos = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    cuts.push(cut[pos][t]);
    pos = cut[pos][t];
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], cutPositions: cuts.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
DP over subsequences with a fixed length captures real-world problems like quantizing sequences while minimizing total variation; it tests state definition, reconstruction, and complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: last index and length; - Transition: O(n) over prior indices; - Reconstruction via parent pointers; - Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubsequence(A, k) {
  const n = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
      for (let j=0;j<i;j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, last = -1;
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (dp[i][k] < best) { best = dp[i][k]; last = i; }
  const idx = Array(k);
  let t = k, cur = last;
  while (t>=1) { idx[t-1] = cur; cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with non-uniform skip constraints and reconstruction, common in scheduling and resource-constrained selection. It ensures you can encode constraints as a forward DP and extract the chosen indices.

## Key Concepts

- DP with forward state (i, r)
- Transition skipping vs taking with jump i+gap[i]
- Path reconstruction and complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumWithGaps(A, gap, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const memo = Array.from({ length: n + 2 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  const go = (i, r) => {
    if (r === 0) return 0;
    if (i > n) return NEG;
    if (memo[i][r] != null) return memo[i][r];
    const skip = go(i + 1, r);
    const take = A[i - 1] + go(Math.min(n + 1, i + gap[i - 1]), r - 1);
    const best = Math.max(skip, take);
    memo[i][r] = best;
    return best;
  };

  const best = go(1, k);
  return { best, memo };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for variable k_max instead of exact k? 
- How would you reconstruct the indices efficiently?

