## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model constraints with DP, including exact count, non-adjacency, and reconstruction; this is a common interview pattern for medium-difficulty tasks.

## Key Concepts

- 2D DP with adjacency constraint
- Exact-count optimization
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPromo(revenue, k, cost){
  const n = revenue.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      // take i
      if (t > 0){
        const takeFrom = i >= 2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t-1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = takeFrom + revenue[i-1] - cost;
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], val);
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i = n, t = k;
  const picks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t >= 0){
    if (dp[i][t] === dp[i-1][t]){ i--; }
    else { picks.unshift(i); i -= 2; t--; }
  }
  return { best: dp[n][k], picks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize space to O(k)?
- How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of dynamic programming on prefixes with a non-crossing pairing constraint. The core challenge is encoding the no-crossing condition by considering the last pair ending at position i with its left partner p.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefixes**: Extra dimension tracks the number of pairs formed
- **Non-crossing constraint**: Enforced by pairing i with p < i and using dp[p-1][t-1], which ensures all pairs lie completely to the left of p
- **Reconstruction**: Requires storing backpointers to output the actual pairs
- **Complexity**: Time O(n²k), Space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPairs(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      prev[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let p = 1; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p - 1][t - 1] + A[p - 1] * A[i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = { skip: false, p };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t].skip) {
      i--;
    } else {
      const p = prev[i][t].p;
      pairs.push([p, i]);
      i = p - 1;
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs: pairs.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to model partitioning with a non-linear block value, a common DP pattern in beginner interviews. Requires prefix sums for O(1) block sums, a clear state definition over prefix length and blocks, and backtracking to output endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP on partitions with exact k blocks
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- State: dp[i][t], parent pointers for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = S[i]*i;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=t;i<=n;i++) {
      for (let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (S[i]-S[p])*(i-p);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i=n, t=k; const ends=[];
  while (t>0) { const p = par[i][t]; ends.push([p+1, i]); i = p; t--; }
  ends.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], windows: ends};
}
```

